{% block onePageContent %}

<p class="govuk-body">You can use this tool to help you keep in touch with people on probation. Before you sign someone up to use online check ins, you should have a conversation with them to explain what online check ins are and how they can benefit from using them.</p>
<h2 class="govuk-heading-l">Important information you should share with the person</h2>
<p class="govuk-body">Using online check ins is optional for people on probation, and not checking in on time is not enforceable.</p>
<p class="govuk-body">You can <a class="govuk-link govuk-link--no-visited-state" href="/practitioner-guidance" rel="noreferrer noopener" target="_blank">check the guidance for probation practitioners to learn more about online check ins (opens in new tab).</a></p>
<p class="govuk-body">If the person would like some time to think about if they'd like to use online check ins, you should offer them <a class="govuk-link govuk-link--no-visited-state" href="/storyboard" rel="noreferrer noopener" target="_blank">this simple overview of online check ins which can also be printed (opens in new tab),</a> and let them decide in their own time.</p>
<p class="govuk-body">If they'd like a <a class="govuk-link govuk-link--no-visited-state" href="/guidance" rel="noreferrer noopener" target="_blank">link to more detailed information for people on probation (opens in new tab)</a>, share this with them.</p>

<h3 class="govuk-heading-m">Explaining the benefits of using online check ins</h3>
<p class="govuk-body">You should explain that online check ins have been designed and tested with people on probation, to make them as easy to use as possible. They have also helped people on probation to:</p>
<ul class="govuk-list govuk-list--bullet">
  <li>complete a course for their job that in person meetings would make it difficult to do</li>
  <li>check in without having to travel into the office if they have caring responsibilities</li>
  <li>save money on travel to probation appointments</li>
  <li>let their probation officer know they need support with something or about something positive that's happened</li>
</ul>
<h3 class="govuk-heading-m">If the person wants to try online check ins before deciding</h3>
<p class="govuk-body">If you or the person you’re signing up to use online check ins would like to try using them while in person, you can choose to schedule check ins one at a time. This allows you to sign the person up and schedule a check in for the same day. They’ll get the link to check in as soon as you’ve scheduled the same day check in.</p>
<p class="govuk-body">If you both decide to keep using online check ins, you can change the frequency of their online check ins within MPOP to a recurring schedule, or you can choose to keep them as one at a time and schedule them as needed.</p>
<p class="govuk-body">If the person tries using online check ins and decides they do not want to continue, you should stop online check ins in their MPOP case file. They’ll get a text or email confirming check ins have stopped.</p>
<h3 class="govuk-heading-m">How to get help if they struggle with technology</h3>
<p class="govuk-body">If they need help using online check ins, they can go to the reception at their probation office and ask to speak to an Engaging People on Probation (EPOP) representative. They may be able to help them with using technology.</p>
<p class="govuk-body">They can also use our new AI chatbot to ask questions they might have about online check ins. The chatbot uses the guidance to help answer questions. We are currently testing this so any feedback from people on probation will help us to understand if it’s working well for them or not.</p>
